\ The mixing of six fixed bowls by changing their temporary order.
\ All sauce operations use exact decimal quantities.
\ A complete round always reads one old snapshot and writes a separate new state.
\ The last visible order is remembered; final rounds do not replace it.
\ This file contains no CAL test results.

A mixing task has
  a number called phase,
  a number called round index,
  a number called position,
  a number called chosen identity,
  a number called previous identity,
  a number called following identity,
  a wide quantity called chosen old,
  a wide quantity called previous old,
  a wide quantity called following old,
  a wide quantity called direct pour,
  a wide quantity called chosen stone,
  a wide quantity called fresh value,
  a wide quantity called shared sauce value.

The mixing task is a mixing task.
The old bowls are a bowl state.
The new bowls are a bowl state.
The last visible bowl order is a bowl order.

\ Taking a snapshot first is vital: no new bowl enters another calculation
\ until the whole round has been completed.
To remember the old bowls:
  Put the current bowls' first bowl into the old bowls' first bowl.
  Put the current bowls' second bowl into the old bowls' second bowl.
  Put the current bowls' third bowl into the old bowls' third bowl.
  Put the current bowls' fourth bowl into the old bowls' fourth bowl.
  Put the current bowls' fifth bowl into the old bowls' fifth bowl.
  Put the current bowls' sixth bowl into the old bowls' sixth bowl.

To commit the new bowls:
  Put the new bowls' first bowl into the current bowls' first bowl.
  Put the new bowls' second bowl into the current bowls' second bowl.
  Put the new bowls' third bowl into the current bowls' third bowl.
  Put the new bowls' fourth bowl into the current bowls' fourth bowl.
  Put the new bowls' fifth bowl into the current bowls' fifth bowl.
  Put the new bowls' sixth bowl into the current bowls' sixth bowl.

\ A bowl's identity is fixed, no matter which position it occupies.
To read the old bowl given a number into a wide quantity:
  If the number is 1, put the old bowls' first bowl into the wide quantity; exit.
  If the number is 2, put the old bowls' second bowl into the wide quantity; exit.
  If the number is 3, put the old bowls' third bowl into the wide quantity; exit.
  If the number is 4, put the old bowls' fourth bowl into the wide quantity; exit.
  If the number is 5, put the old bowls' fifth bowl into the wide quantity; exit.
  If the number is 6, put the old bowls' sixth bowl into the wide quantity; exit.

\ Positions refer to the CURRENT chosen permutation, not fixed identities.
To read the ordered identity given a number into another number:
  If the number is 1, put the chosen bowl order's first identity into the other number; exit.
  If the number is 2, put the chosen bowl order's second identity into the other number; exit.
  If the number is 3, put the chosen bowl order's third identity into the other number; exit.
  If the number is 4, put the chosen bowl order's fourth identity into the other number; exit.
  If the number is 5, put the chosen bowl order's fifth identity into the other number; exit.
  If the number is 6, put the chosen bowl order's sixth identity into the other number; exit.

\ Record the last VISIBLE order before the twelve final rounds change the
\ working order. Seal questions will refer to this saved order later.
To remember the final visible order:
  Put the chosen bowl order's first identity into the last visible bowl order's first identity.
  Put the chosen bowl order's second identity into the last visible bowl order's second identity.
  Put the chosen bowl order's third identity into the last visible bowl order's third identity.
  Put the chosen bowl order's fourth identity into the last visible bowl order's fourth identity.
  Put the chosen bowl order's fifth identity into the last visible bowl order's fifth identity.
  Put the chosen bowl order's sixth identity into the last visible bowl order's sixth identity.

\ This is the only routine that writes one new bowl by its fixed identity.
To store the mixed bowl:
  If the mixing task's chosen identity is 1, put the mixing task's fresh value into the new bowls' first bowl; exit.
  If the mixing task's chosen identity is 2, put the mixing task's fresh value into the new bowls' second bowl; exit.
  If the mixing task's chosen identity is 3, put the mixing task's fresh value into the new bowls' third bowl; exit.
  If the mixing task's chosen identity is 4, put the mixing task's fresh value into the new bowls' fourth bowl; exit.
  If the mixing task's chosen identity is 5, put the mixing task's fresh value into the new bowls' fifth bowl; exit.
  If the mixing task's chosen identity is 6, put the mixing task's fresh value into the new bowls' sixth bowl; exit.

\ Find current, previous and next bowls in the circular chosen order.
\ Position 1 follows position 6. Position 6 precedes position 1.
To find the three neighboring bowls:
  Put the mixing task's position into a position number.
  Read the ordered identity given the position number into an identity number.
  Put the identity number into the mixing task's chosen identity.
  Put the position number into a previous position number.
  If the previous position number is 1, put 7 into the previous position number.
  Subtract 1 from the previous position number.
  Read the ordered identity given the previous position number into the identity number.
  Put the identity number into the mixing task's previous identity.
  Put the position number into a following position number.
  Add 1 to the following position number.
  If the following position number is greater than 6, put 1 into the following position number.
  Read the ordered identity given the following position number into the identity number.
  Put the identity number into the mixing task's following identity.
  Read the old bowl given the mixing task's chosen identity into the mixing task's chosen old.
  Read the old bowl given the mixing task's previous identity into the mixing task's previous old.
  Read the old bowl given the mixing task's following identity into the mixing task's following old.

\ Visible drop positions 1..6 use wheat, barley, salt, bitter, red, wheat.
To select the stone in a mixing position:
  If the mixing task's position is 1, put the current stones' wheat into the mixing task's chosen stone; exit.
  If the mixing task's position is 2, put the current stones' barley into the mixing task's chosen stone; exit.
  If the mixing task's position is 3, put the current stones' salt into the mixing task's chosen stone; exit.
  If the mixing task's position is 4, put the current stones' bitter into the mixing task's chosen stone; exit.
  If the mixing task's position is 5, put the current stones' red into the mixing task's chosen stone; exit.
  If the mixing task's position is 6, put the current stones' wheat into the mixing task's chosen stone; exit.

\ The first three positions get one direct pour each.
\ These pours are saved quantities ADDED to u; the old bowls do not change.
\ The factors for positions 1, 2 and 3 are 3*i, 5*i and 7*i.
To calculate the direct visible pour:
  Put "0" into the mixing task's direct pour.
  If the mixing task's position is greater than 3, exit.
  Put the last visible drop into a poured wide quantity.
  Multiply the last visible drop by the poured wide quantity.
  Put the mixing task's chosen stone into a multiplied stone wide quantity.
  Multiply the mixing task's chosen old by the multiplied stone wide quantity.
  Add the multiplied stone wide quantity to the poured wide quantity.
  Put the mixing task's position into a factor number.
  Multiply the factor number by 2.
  Add 1 to the factor number.
  Multiply the factor number by the visible work's index.
  Add the factor number to the poured wide quantity.
  Keep the poured wide quantity for the sauce.
  Put the poured wide quantity into the mixing task's direct pour.

\ Visible mixing: u=old(B)+2*old(P)+3*old(N)+directPour+D+stone(q).
\ new(B)=SAVE(u*u+5*old(P)*old(N)+i*q).
To perform visible bowl mixing:
  Find the three neighboring bowls.
  Select the stone in a mixing position.
  Calculate the direct visible pour.
  Put the mixing task's chosen old into the mixing task's fresh value.
  Put the mixing task's previous old into a weighted previous wide quantity.
  Multiply the weighted previous wide quantity by 2.
  Add the weighted previous wide quantity to the mixing task's fresh value.
  Put the mixing task's following old into a weighted following wide quantity.
  Multiply the weighted following wide quantity by 3.
  Add the weighted following wide quantity to the mixing task's fresh value.
  Add the mixing task's direct pour to the mixing task's fresh value.
  Add the last visible drop to the mixing task's fresh value.
  Add the mixing task's chosen stone to the mixing task's fresh value.
  Put the mixing task's fresh value into a result wide quantity.
  Multiply the mixing task's fresh value by the result wide quantity.
  Put the mixing task's previous old into a neighbor product wide quantity.
  Multiply the mixing task's following old by the neighbor product wide quantity.
  Multiply the neighbor product wide quantity by 5.
  Add the neighbor product wide quantity to the result wide quantity.
  Put the visible work's index into a term number.
  Multiply the term number by the mixing task's position.
  Add the term number to the result wide quantity.
  Keep the result wide quantity for the sauce.
  Put the result wide quantity into the mixing task's fresh value.
  Store the mixed bowl.

\ A final mixing round must use SAVED S_r both to choose the order and in u.
\ u=old(B)+3*old(P)+5*old(N)+S_r+r+q*q.
\ new(B)=SAVE(u*u+7*old(P)*old(N)).
To perform final bowl mixing:
  Find the three neighboring bowls.
  Put the mixing task's chosen old into the mixing task's fresh value.
  Put the mixing task's previous old into a weighted previous wide quantity.
  Multiply the weighted previous wide quantity by 3.
  Add the weighted previous wide quantity to the mixing task's fresh value.
  Put the mixing task's following old into a weighted following wide quantity.
  Multiply the weighted following wide quantity by 5.
  Add the weighted following wide quantity to the mixing task's fresh value.
  Add the mixing task's shared sauce value to the mixing task's fresh value.
  Add the mixing task's round index to the mixing task's fresh value.
  Put the mixing task's position into a squared position number.
  Multiply the squared position number by the mixing task's position.
  Add the squared position number to the mixing task's fresh value.
  Put the mixing task's fresh value into a result wide quantity.
  Multiply the mixing task's fresh value by the result wide quantity.
  Put the mixing task's previous old into a neighbor product wide quantity.
  Multiply the mixing task's following old by the neighbor product wide quantity.
  Multiply the neighbor product wide quantity by 7.
  Add the neighbor product wide quantity to the result wide quantity.
  Keep the result wide quantity for the sauce.
  Put the result wide quantity into the mixing task's fresh value.
  Store the mixed bowl.

\ Six results are computed from the SAME old snapshot, never in place.
To finish one mixing round:
  Put 1 into the mixing task's position.
  Loop.
    If the mixing task's position is greater than 6, break.
    If the mixing task's phase is 1, perform visible bowl mixing.
    If the mixing task's phase is 2, perform final bowl mixing.
    Add 1 to the mixing task's position.
  Repeat.
  Commit the new bowls.

\ The stone row and drop D have already been computed for this index.
To mix bowls for the visible drop:
  Put 1 into the mixing task's phase.
  Choose a bowl order from the last visible drop.
  If the visible work's index is 46, remember the final visible order.
  Remember the old bowls.
  Finish one mixing round.

\ Final round r begins by saving S_r=SAVE(sum(old bowls)+149*r).
\ Both the permutation and all six u expressions use this one value.
To prepare the final round given a number:
  Put the number into the mixing task's round index.
  Remember the old bowls.
  Put "0" into the mixing task's shared sauce value.
  Add the old bowls' first bowl to the mixing task's shared sauce value.
  Add the old bowls' second bowl to the mixing task's shared sauce value.
  Add the old bowls' third bowl to the mixing task's shared sauce value.
  Add the old bowls' fourth bowl to the mixing task's shared sauce value.
  Add the old bowls' fifth bowl to the mixing task's shared sauce value.
  Add the old bowls' sixth bowl to the mixing task's shared sauce value.
  Put the mixing task's round index into a weighted round number.
  Multiply the weighted round number by 149.
  Add the weighted round number to the mixing task's shared sauce value.
  Keep the mixing task's shared sauce value for the sauce.
  Choose a bowl order from the mixing task's shared sauce value.
  Put 2 into the mixing task's phase.

To finish one final round given a number:
  Prepare the final round given the number.
  Finish one mixing round.

To finish twelve final rounds:
  Put 1 into a final round number.
  Loop.
    If the final round number is greater than 12, break.
    Finish one final round given the final round number.
    Add 1 to the final round number.
  Repeat.

\ Prepare the sauce input first. This routine is shared by the regression
\ checks and the future complete date conversion.
To make the sauce through the visible drops:
  Make all seven hidden drops.
  Prepare the previous drops.
  Initialize the six bowls.
  Put 1 into a visible position number.
  Loop.
    If the visible position number is greater than 46, break.
    Calculate visible drop given the visible position number.
    Mix bowls for the visible drop.
    Add 1 to the visible position number.
  Repeat.

To make the complete sauce:
  Make the sauce through the visible drops.
  Finish twelve final rounds.
